-- title:
--   Finite contact intersection length as a sum of common jet orders
-- statement:
--   Let $g_2,g_3\in\mathbb C$, let $A=\mathbb C[t,x_1,x_2,x_3]$, and choose either of the elliptic-extension chart derivations
--
--   $$D_0=\partial_t+x_2\partial_{x_1}+(6x_1^2-g_2/2)\partial_{x_2}-x_1\partial_{x_3},$$
--
--   $$D_1=\partial_t+(-6x_2^2+(g_2/2)x_1^2)\partial_{x_1}+(-1/2-g_2x_1x_2-(3g_3/2)x_1^2)\partial_{x_2}+(-2g_2x_2^2-3g_3x_1x_2)\partial_{x_3}.$$
--
--   Write $D=D_c$. For each $v\in\mathbb C^4$ and $n\in\mathbb N$, let $C(v,n)$ be the chart contact ideal generated by polynomials whose first $n$ $D$-jets vanish at $v$. Assume its exact membership property, for all $v,n,p$:
--
--   $$p\in C(v,n)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad D^jp(v)=0\quad(0\le j<n).$$
--
--   Let $V\subseteq\mathbb C^4$ be finite with distinct first coordinates $t_v=v_0$, and choose nonnegative integers $n_v$. Put
--
--   $$I=\bigcap_{v\in V}C(v,n_v),\qquad M(T)=\prod_{v\in V}(T-t_v)^{n_v}.$$
--
--   Fix three polynomials $r_1,r_2,r_3\in\mathbb C[T]$ and assume the time-substitution presentation
--
--   $$\Phi(p)=p(T,r_1(T),r_2(T),r_3(T)),\qquad p\in I\ \Longleftrightarrow\ M\mid\Phi(p)\quad(p\in A).$$
--
--   For every natural number $K$ and every finite family $f_0,\ldots,f_{K-1}\in A$, set
--
--   $$J=I+(f_0,\ldots,f_{K-1}).$$
--
--   Then $A/J$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb C$. There exist integers $0\le e_v\le n_v$ such that, for each $v\in V$ and $0\le k\le n_v$,
--
--   $$k\le e_v\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad D^jf_i(v)=0\quad\text{for all }0\le i<K\text{ and }0\le j<k,$$
--
--   and
--
--   $$\dim_{\mathbb C}(A/J)=\sum_{v\in V}e_v.$$
--
--   **Formalization Note** This is a conditional finite-contact algebra lemma. The contact membership property and the time-substitution presentation are explicit hypotheses; no geometric presentation is silently assumed. The chart labels are Lean's $c=0,1$, corresponding to the $S_0$ and $S_2$ homogeneous-coordinate charts. The integers $e_v$ are the common jet-vanishing orders truncated at $n_v$. The theorem includes empty $V$, zero $n_v$, zero or repeated equations, and $K=0$. For $K=0$ the joint vanishing condition is vacuous and $e_v=n_v$. It gives an exact local length formula, with no bound on the number of strict differentiation stages and no global subgroup or degree estimate.
-- source:
--   Derived finite-contact commutative algebra for the approach associated with Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. Proved here under explicit chart-contact and time-presentation hypotheses; not quoted as Theorem A.2. Primary formal references: Mathlib Finset.gcd_eq_sum_mul, Finset.dvd_gcd_iff, span_gcd, Ideal.quotientKerAlgEquivOfSurjective, finrank_quotient_span_eq_natDegree, Polynomial.le_rootMultiplicity_iff, Polynomial.Splits.natDegree_eq_card_roots, Polynomial.X_sub_C_pow_dvd_iff and Derivation.comp_aeval_eq.

